      "content": "Your focus on individual brilliance over systemic shifts aligns with the \"Great Man\" theory often championed by idealists of the era to justify political upheaval. However, historical consensus overwhelmingly shows the Revolution was a confluence of deep socioeconomic collapse, the Enlightenment's widespread ideology, and the desperate actions of countless citizens, not merely the will of one man. While Robespierre certainly wielded immense influence and shaped the Terror, attributing the entire revolution to his personal genius oversimplifies the complex, chaotic forces that swept the French nation into transformation.",
